dict(slug="osobuco-braseado-polenta-cremosa-gremolata", cat="carnes", fecha="2026-10-28",
 img="https://images.pexels.com/photos/9919629/pexels-photo-9919629.jpeg", foto="Pexels",
 es=dict(
  titulo="Osobuco braseado con polenta cremosa y gremolata",
  bajada="La receta de Cocina 2 de la escuela: dos horas de braseado lento, una salsa que sale de la misma olla y una gremolata que corta la intensidad.",
  cuerpo=[
   ("p","El osobuco es un corte duro, con mucho colágeno y un hueso con médula en el centro. Por eso no se apura: se <strong>brasea</strong>, es decir, se sella primero y después se cocina tapado, a fuego bajo y con poco líquido, hasta que la carne se separa sola. En la escuela lo hicimos en la clase 4 de Cocina 2 con polenta cremosa y gremolata."),
   ("h2","Ingredientes (2 porciones)"),
   ("tabla",["Ingrediente","Cantidad"],[
     ["Osobuco","2 piezas de unos 350 g"],
     ["Aceite y manteca","20 cc y 20 g"],
     ["Pimentón ahumado","5 g"],
     ["Cebolla chica, ajo, apio","1 cebolla, 1 diente, 1 rama"],
     ["Zanahoria y puerro","½ de cada uno"],
     ["Vino tinto","100 cc"],
     ["Fondo de res","700 cc"],
     ["Salvia, orégano y perejil","1 ramita de cada uno"],
     ["Sal y pimienta","A gusto"],
   ]),
   ("h2","Paso a paso del braseado"),
   ("ol",["Cortá todos los vegetales en macedonia (cubos chicos y parejos) y reservalos.","Salpimentá el osobuco y sellalo en aceite y manteca hasta dorarlo bien de los dos lados. Agregá los vegetales a la misma olla.","Dorá todo y desglasá con el vino tinto, raspando el fondo.","Dejá reducir hasta que se evapore el alcohol y sumá el fondo de res y las hierbas.","Tapá y cociná al menos 2 horas a fuego bajo, o en el horno a 170 °C. A mitad de cocción, dá vuelta la carne.","Cuando esté tierno, retiralo y tapalo con papel aluminio.","Pisá con un pisapuré los vegetales dentro del líquido de cocción, colá y reducí a fuego fuerte. Rectificá la sal.","Volvé a poner el osobuco en la salsa reducida para que se impregne."]),
   ("tip","El sellado no es para \"cerrar los jugos\" (eso es un mito): es para dorar. Ese dorado es el sabor que después levanta el vino al desglasar."),
   ("h2","La polenta cremosa"),
   ("p","Para 2 porciones: 50 g de harina de maíz precocida, 200 cc de leche, 100 cc de crema, 1 diente de ajo hecho pasta, 4 g de perejil picado y 20 g de manteca. Se hierven la leche y la crema con el ajo y el perejil, se agrega la polenta en lluvia revolviendo sin parar y se cocina a fuego mínimo hasta que tenga textura de puré cremoso. La manteca va al final, fuera del fuego, y solo si se sirve en el momento."),
   ("h2","La gremolata"),
   ("p","Es el toque fresco que equilibra un plato tan intenso: 50 g de perejil, 1 diente de ajo, 10 g de alcaparras, ralladura y jugo de 1 limón, 30 cc de aceite de oliva, sal y pimienta. Se pica todo, se mezcla y se suma el aceite. Tiene que quedar parecida a un chimichurri, pero menos fluida."),
   ("dato","El braseado mejora de un día para el otro: el colágeno gelifica la salsa y los sabores se asientan. Es un plato ideal para dejar hecho antes de recibir."),
   ("p","Es uno de esos platos que en un menú de invierno se terminan en la mesa: la carne se desarma con la cuchara."),
  ]),
 en=dict(
  titulo="Braised osso buco with creamy polenta and gremolata",
  bajada="The Cooking 2 recipe from culinary school: two hours of slow braising, a sauce made in the same pot and a gremolata that cuts through the richness.",
  cuerpo=[
   ("p","Osso buco is a tough cut, full of collagen, with a marrow bone in the center. That's why it can't be rushed: it's <strong>braised</strong>, meaning it's seared first and then cooked covered, over low heat with little liquid, until the meat falls off on its own. At school we made it in class 4 of Cooking 2 with creamy polenta and gremolata."),
   ("h2","Ingredients (2 servings)"),
   ("tabla",["Ingredient","Amount"],[
     ["Osso buco","2 pieces of about 350 g"],
     ["Oil and butter","20 ml and 20 g"],
     ["Smoked paprika","5 g"],
     ["Small onion, garlic, celery","1 onion, 1 clove, 1 stalk"],
     ["Carrot and leek","½ of each"],
     ["Red wine","100 ml"],
     ["Beef stock","700 ml"],
     ["Sage, oregano and parsley","1 sprig of each"],
     ["Salt and pepper","To taste"],
   ]),
   ("h2","Braising step by step"),
   ("ol",["Cut all the vegetables into small, even dice and set aside.","Season the osso buco and sear it in oil and butter until well browned on both sides. Add the vegetables to the same pot.","Brown everything and deglaze with the red wine, scraping the bottom.","Let it reduce until the alcohol evaporates, then add the beef stock and the herbs.","Cover and cook for at least 2 hours over low heat, or in the oven at 170 °C. Turn the meat halfway through.","When tender, remove it and cover with foil.","Mash the vegetables inside the cooking liquid with a potato masher, strain and reduce over high heat. Adjust the salt.","Return the osso buco to the reduced sauce so it soaks up the flavor."]),
   ("tip","Searing doesn't \"seal in the juices\" (that's a myth): it's for browning. That browning is the flavor the wine lifts when you deglaze."),
   ("h2","Creamy polenta"),
   ("p","For 2 servings: 50 g instant cornmeal, 200 ml milk, 100 ml cream, 1 garlic clove mashed to a paste, 4 g chopped parsley and 20 g butter. Bring the milk and cream to a boil with the garlic and parsley, add the polenta in a thin stream while whisking constantly, and cook over the lowest heat until it has a creamy mash texture. The butter goes in at the end, off the heat, and only if you serve it right away."),
   ("h2","Gremolata"),
   ("p","It's the fresh touch that balances such a rich dish: 50 g parsley, 1 garlic clove, 10 g capers, the zest and juice of 1 lemon, 30 ml olive oil, salt and pepper. Chop everything, mix and add the oil. It should look like a chimichurri, only less runny."),
   ("dato","Braises get better overnight: the collagen sets the sauce and the flavors settle. It's an ideal dish to make ahead before having people over."),
   ("p","It's one of those winter-menu dishes that gets finished at the table: the meat falls apart with a spoon."),
  ])),
